1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,426
The nose of a Japanese bullet train

2
00:00:02,426 --> 00:00:04,714
can be longer than a whole subway

3
00:00:04,714 --> 00:00:04,992
car.

4
00:00:05,172 --> 00:00:06,411
On some trains,

5
00:00:06,411 --> 00:00:08,643
a big part of the first car

6
00:00:08,643 --> 00:00:09,717
is just nose,

7
00:00:09,717 --> 00:00:11,453
with no seats inside.

8
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:13,748
That looks like a waste of space

9
00:00:13,748 --> 00:00:15,996
on the most crowded railway in the

10
00:00:15,996 --> 00:00:16,393
world.

11
00:00:16,573 --> 00:00:18,054
But without that nose,

12
00:00:18,054 --> 00:00:20,612
every time the train entered a tunnel,

13
00:00:20,612 --> 00:00:23,170
people living near the exit would hear

14
00:00:23,170 --> 00:00:24,584
a bang like a cannon.

15
00:00:24,764 --> 00:00:27,975
Today, ten parts of the bullet train,

16
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:30,058
and why each one exists.

17
00:00:30,407 --> 00:00:33,923
Japan opened the first Shinkansen line,

18
00:00:33,923 --> 00:00:36,087
between Tokyo and Osaka,

19
00:00:36,087 --> 00:00:37,620
on October first,

20
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:42,308
nineteen sixty-four, just before the Tokyo Olympics.

21
00:00:42,488 --> 00:00:45,010
It was the first high speed railway

22
00:00:45,010 --> 00:00:45,947
in the world.

23
00:00:46,127 --> 00:00:49,756
Since then, the Shinkansen has carried billions

24
00:00:49,756 --> 00:00:52,457
of passengers, and it is famous for

25
00:00:52,457 --> 00:00:53,384
two numbers.

26
00:00:53,564 --> 00:00:56,463
An average delay of under a minute

27
00:00:56,463 --> 00:00:59,788
per train, and no passenger deaths from

28
00:00:59,788 --> 00:01:03,711
derailments or collisions in normal operation.

29
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:06,888
As always, every fact comes from a

30
00:01:06,888 --> 00:01:10,297
named source, Japanese railway companies,

31
00:01:10,297 --> 00:01:13,872
engineering papers, and railway references,

32
00:01:13,872 --> 00:01:16,367
all listed in the description.

33
00:01:16,547 --> 00:01:19,241
Let's start with the most famous part,

34
00:01:19,241 --> 00:01:19,879
the nose.

35
00:01:20,229 --> 00:01:23,074
Why do bullet trains have such long

36
00:01:23,074 --> 00:01:23,562
noses?

37
00:01:23,742 --> 00:01:26,214
The main reason is not speed.

38
00:01:26,394 --> 00:01:27,579
It is tunnels.

39
00:01:27,759 --> 00:01:30,163
When a fast train enters a tunnel,

40
00:01:30,163 --> 00:01:32,214
it pushes the air ahead of it

41
00:01:32,214 --> 00:01:33,204
like a piston.

42
00:01:33,384 --> 00:01:35,986
That creates a pressure wave that races

43
00:01:35,986 --> 00:01:38,254
through the tunnel at the speed of

44
00:01:38,254 --> 00:01:38,655
sound.

45
00:01:38,835 --> 00:01:40,955
When it reaches the other end,

46
00:01:40,955 --> 00:01:42,793
part of it bursts out as a

47
00:01:42,793 --> 00:01:45,055
loud boom, called a tunnel boom,

48
00:01:45,055 --> 00:01:47,812
which can rattle windows of houses near

49
00:01:47,812 --> 00:01:48,448
the exit.

50
00:01:48,797 --> 00:01:52,057
A long, slowly tapering nose pushes the

51
00:01:52,057 --> 00:01:53,394
air more gently,

52
00:01:53,394 --> 00:01:56,486
so the pressure rises more slowly and

53
00:01:56,486 --> 00:01:58,910
the boom becomes much weaker.

54
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:01,501
That is why the noses got longer

55
00:02:01,501 --> 00:02:03,385
as the trains got faster.

56
00:02:03,565 --> 00:02:05,553
The newest test train,

57
00:02:05,553 --> 00:02:09,076
the ALFA-X, has a nose about twenty-two

58
00:02:09,076 --> 00:02:11,154
meters long on one end,

59
00:02:11,154 --> 00:02:14,226
almost as long as an entire normal

60
00:02:14,226 --> 00:02:15,129
train car.

61
00:02:15,478 --> 00:02:18,035
The most famous nose was inspired by

62
00:02:18,035 --> 00:02:18,532
a bird.

63
00:02:18,712 --> 00:02:21,009
In the nineteen nineties,

64
00:02:21,009 --> 00:02:24,317
Eiji Nakatsu, an engineer at JR West

65
00:02:24,317 --> 00:02:26,063
and a bird watcher,

66
00:02:26,063 --> 00:02:29,922
noticed that the kingfisher dives from air

67
00:02:29,922 --> 00:02:32,954
into water with almost no splash,

68
00:02:32,954 --> 00:02:34,700
thanks to its long,

69
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:35,895
pointed beak.

70
00:02:36,075 --> 00:02:38,422
The nose of the five hundred series

71
00:02:38,422 --> 00:02:39,697
was shaped like it.

72
00:02:39,877 --> 00:02:42,694
Reports say the new train was quieter,

73
00:02:42,694 --> 00:02:45,957
used about fifteen percent less electricity,

74
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:48,329
and ran faster than the old one.

75
00:02:48,678 --> 00:02:51,936
Why do many Shinkansen tunnel entrances have

76
00:02:51,936 --> 00:02:54,378
a strange box sticking out of the

77
00:02:54,378 --> 00:02:57,340
mountain, often with holes in its sides?

78
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:59,332
It is a tunnel hood,

79
00:02:59,332 --> 00:03:01,235
also called a buffer.

80
00:03:01,415 --> 00:03:04,073
It works together with the long nose.

81
00:03:04,253 --> 00:03:06,849
When the train enters the hood,

82
00:03:06,849 --> 00:03:09,779
some of the air escapes through the

83
00:03:09,779 --> 00:03:13,044
openings, so the pressure in the tunnel

84
00:03:13,044 --> 00:03:15,723
rises in steps instead of all at

85
00:03:15,723 --> 00:03:16,142
once.

86
00:03:16,322 --> 00:03:18,337
The pressure wave is softer,

87
00:03:18,337 --> 00:03:20,279
and the boom at the far end

88
00:03:20,279 --> 00:03:21,070
is quieter.

89
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:24,609
Japan needed this more than most countries,

90
00:03:24,609 --> 00:03:27,873
because its high speed lines cross mountains

91
00:03:27,873 --> 00:03:29,652
and are full of tunnels,

92
00:03:29,652 --> 00:03:32,322
and many tunnels are close to homes.

93
00:03:32,502 --> 00:03:35,736
Japanese rules limit the noise and pressure

94
00:03:35,736 --> 00:03:38,067
that a train can cause near the

95
00:03:38,067 --> 00:03:41,225
line, so engineers attack the problem from

96
00:03:41,225 --> 00:03:44,008
both sides, the train and the tunnel.

97
00:03:44,357 --> 00:03:47,097
Even the inside of the tunnel matters.

98
00:03:47,277 --> 00:03:50,031
Some tunnels were built with a larger

99
00:03:50,031 --> 00:03:52,487
cross section so the air has more

100
00:03:52,487 --> 00:03:54,719
room to move around the train.

101
00:03:54,899 --> 00:03:57,432
A bigger tunnel costs much more to

102
00:03:57,432 --> 00:03:59,816
dig, but a smaller one makes the

103
00:03:59,816 --> 00:04:02,945
boom louder and the ride less comfortable.

104
00:04:03,125 --> 00:04:04,615
In high speed rail,

105
00:04:04,615 --> 00:04:06,811
air is the enemy you have to

106
00:04:06,811 --> 00:04:07,908
design around.

107
00:04:08,258 --> 00:04:10,341
Why was part of a bullet train

108
00:04:10,341 --> 00:04:11,660
copied from an owl?

109
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:13,953
The part is the pantograph,

110
00:04:13,953 --> 00:04:16,457
the arm on the roof that touches

111
00:04:16,457 --> 00:04:19,665
the overhead wire to collect electricity.

112
00:04:19,845 --> 00:04:22,626
At three hundred kilometers an hour,

113
00:04:22,626 --> 00:04:25,639
air rushing past it creates loud noise.

114
00:04:25,819 --> 00:04:28,136
Eiji Nakatsu looked at owls,

115
00:04:28,136 --> 00:04:30,370
which hunt in near silence.

116
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:33,818
Their wing feathers have small comb-like edges

117
00:04:33,818 --> 00:04:36,376
that break the air into tiny swirls,

118
00:04:36,376 --> 00:04:38,224
making their flight quiet.

119
00:04:38,573 --> 00:04:41,752
The pantograph of the five hundred series

120
00:04:41,752 --> 00:04:45,551
used a wing-shaped support with small serrations,

121
00:04:45,551 --> 00:04:47,954
inspired by those owl feathers.

122
00:04:48,134 --> 00:04:51,892
The serrations break the large noisy swirls

123
00:04:51,892 --> 00:04:53,639
of air into smaller,

124
00:04:53,639 --> 00:04:54,775
quieter ones.

125
00:04:54,955 --> 00:04:58,115
Combined with a smaller design and covers

126
00:04:58,115 --> 00:04:59,811
around the pantograph,

127
00:04:59,811 --> 00:05:02,894
the noise dropped enough to meet Japan's

128
00:05:02,894 --> 00:05:04,975
strict rules at high speed.

129
00:05:05,324 --> 00:05:09,877
Modern Shinkansen trains use fewer pantographs than

130
00:05:09,877 --> 00:05:13,806
older ones, often with streamlined covers or

131
00:05:13,806 --> 00:05:15,770
shields at their base,

132
00:05:15,770 --> 00:05:19,698
because each pantograph adds noise and drag.

133
00:05:19,878 --> 00:05:21,364
The goal is simple.

134
00:05:21,544 --> 00:05:24,550
Collect electricity from a wire at three

135
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:26,579
hundred kilometers an hour,

136
00:05:26,579 --> 00:05:28,984
and do it so quietly that people

137
00:05:28,984 --> 00:05:31,540
living next to the line can sleep.

138
00:05:31,889 --> 00:05:35,002
Why are bullet train cars sealed almost

139
00:05:35,002 --> 00:05:36,359
like an airplane?

140
00:05:36,539 --> 00:05:38,954
Because of the same tunnel pressure.

141
00:05:39,134 --> 00:05:41,602
When a train enters a tunnel at

142
00:05:41,602 --> 00:05:45,105
high speed, the air pressure outside changes

143
00:05:45,105 --> 00:05:45,822
suddenly.

144
00:05:46,002 --> 00:05:47,697
In an ordinary train,

145
00:05:47,697 --> 00:05:50,847
that change would rush in through doors

146
00:05:50,847 --> 00:05:53,835
and gaps, and passengers would feel a

147
00:05:53,835 --> 00:05:55,935
painful pop in their ears,

148
00:05:55,935 --> 00:05:56,985
every tunnel.

149
00:05:57,165 --> 00:05:59,806
So Shinkansen cars are built to be

150
00:05:59,806 --> 00:06:04,699
airtight, with pressure-tight doors and controlled ventilation.

151
00:06:05,049 --> 00:06:08,878
The ventilation system controls how quickly the

152
00:06:08,878 --> 00:06:10,426
air inside changes,

153
00:06:10,426 --> 00:06:13,196
so the pressure in the cabin moves

154
00:06:13,196 --> 00:06:14,663
slowly and gently.

155
00:06:14,843 --> 00:06:17,441
The car body must also be strong

156
00:06:17,441 --> 00:06:20,689
enough to handle the pressure pushing on

157
00:06:20,689 --> 00:06:22,313
it, again and again,

158
00:06:22,313 --> 00:06:25,318
every time the train enters or leaves

159
00:06:25,318 --> 00:06:28,241
a tunnel, or passes another train in

160
00:06:28,241 --> 00:06:28,972
a tunnel.

161
00:06:29,152 --> 00:06:31,903
It is a pressure vessel on wheels.

162
00:06:32,253 --> 00:06:35,298
Passing another train at high speed is

163
00:06:35,298 --> 00:06:37,060
also a pressure event.

164
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:40,290
Two trains approaching each other at three

165
00:06:40,290 --> 00:06:43,195
hundred kilometers an hour each create a

166
00:06:43,195 --> 00:06:45,519
sudden push of air that hits the

167
00:06:45,519 --> 00:06:47,480
windows of the other train.

168
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:50,543
That is one reason the space between

169
00:06:50,543 --> 00:06:53,347
tracks on high speed lines is wider

170
00:06:53,347 --> 00:06:54,789
than on old lines.

171
00:06:54,969 --> 00:06:56,675
The air needs room too.

172
00:06:57,025 --> 00:06:59,773
Why do some bullet trains lean into

173
00:06:59,773 --> 00:07:00,323
curves?

174
00:07:00,503 --> 00:07:03,031
The old Tokaido Shinkansen line,

175
00:07:03,031 --> 00:07:05,401
built in the nineteen sixties,

176
00:07:05,401 --> 00:07:08,324
has many curves that are tighter than

177
00:07:08,324 --> 00:07:10,220
modern high speed lines.

178
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,067
To go faster on it,

179
00:07:12,067 --> 00:07:15,136
the N seven hundred series uses air

180
00:07:15,136 --> 00:07:17,854
springs to tilt the car body by

181
00:07:17,854 --> 00:07:20,222
about one degree in curves.

182
00:07:20,402 --> 00:07:22,059
It is a small angle,

183
00:07:22,059 --> 00:07:24,626
but it lets the train take many

184
00:07:24,626 --> 00:07:28,187
curves at two hundred seventy kilometers an

185
00:07:28,187 --> 00:07:31,334
hour instead of slowing down to around

186
00:07:31,334 --> 00:07:32,825
two hundred fifty.

187
00:07:33,174 --> 00:07:35,241
The tilt is not for the train.

188
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:37,232
It is for the passengers.

189
00:07:37,412 --> 00:07:38,747
In a fast curve,

190
00:07:38,747 --> 00:07:40,748
you are pushed sideways,

191
00:07:40,748 --> 00:07:43,334
and if the force is too strong,

192
00:07:43,334 --> 00:07:46,503
coffee spills and people lose balance.

193
00:07:46,683 --> 00:07:49,143
Tilting the body moves the force so

194
00:07:49,143 --> 00:07:51,182
that it feels more like it is

195
00:07:51,182 --> 00:07:53,080
pushing you into your seat.

196
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,632
The train is not more stable when

197
00:07:55,632 --> 00:07:56,279
it tilts.

198
00:07:56,459 --> 00:07:57,979
It is more comfortable.

199
00:07:58,329 --> 00:08:01,174
Saving a few seconds on every curve

200
00:08:01,174 --> 00:08:01,824
adds up.

201
00:08:02,004 --> 00:08:05,377
Along the busy route between Tokyo and

202
00:08:05,377 --> 00:08:09,549
Osaka, faster curves helped trains save several

203
00:08:09,549 --> 00:08:10,259
minutes.

204
00:08:10,439 --> 00:08:12,330
On a line that runs a train

205
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:13,591
every few minutes,

206
00:08:13,591 --> 00:08:14,642
minutes matter.

207
00:08:14,822 --> 00:08:17,189
The fastest way to speed up an

208
00:08:17,189 --> 00:08:19,871
old railway is sometimes not a new

209
00:08:19,871 --> 00:08:22,159
line, but a train that leans.

210
00:08:22,509 --> 00:08:25,276
Why does Japan spray water on bullet

211
00:08:25,276 --> 00:08:27,351
train tracks when it snows?

212
00:08:27,531 --> 00:08:29,930
Because snow can stick to the bottom

213
00:08:29,930 --> 00:08:31,929
of the train at high speed and

214
00:08:31,929 --> 00:08:34,195
build up into heavy chunks of ice.

215
00:08:34,375 --> 00:08:37,435
Later, when the train reaches a warmer

216
00:08:37,435 --> 00:08:39,529
area, the chunks fall off,

217
00:08:39,529 --> 00:08:41,783
hit the stones of the track,

218
00:08:41,783 --> 00:08:44,441
and throw them up into the train,

219
00:08:44,441 --> 00:08:46,937
breaking windows and equipment.

220
00:08:47,117 --> 00:08:49,610
Wetting the snow makes it heavy and

221
00:08:49,610 --> 00:08:51,889
less likely to fly up and stick.

222
00:08:52,238 --> 00:08:56,439
The Tokaido Shinkansen passes near Sekigahara,

223
00:08:56,439 --> 00:08:59,087
an area known for heavy snow,

224
00:08:59,087 --> 00:09:02,192
and it uses sprinklers to keep the

225
00:09:02,192 --> 00:09:03,105
snow damp.

226
00:09:03,285 --> 00:09:05,786
On lines in the snowiest parts of

227
00:09:05,786 --> 00:09:09,121
Japan, some sprinklers spray warmed water to

228
00:09:09,121 --> 00:09:10,182
melt the snow.

229
00:09:10,362 --> 00:09:13,725
Trains in snowy regions also have plows

230
00:09:13,725 --> 00:09:16,916
and special shapes to throw snow away

231
00:09:16,916 --> 00:09:18,210
from the train.

232
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:20,138
And at some stations,

233
00:09:20,138 --> 00:09:22,919
workers remove ice from the bottom of

234
00:09:22,919 --> 00:09:25,474
trains by hand during short stops,

235
00:09:25,474 --> 00:09:28,104
sometimes with high pressure water.

236
00:09:28,284 --> 00:09:31,279
In winter, keeping a bullet train on

237
00:09:31,279 --> 00:09:34,108
time is not only about engines and

238
00:09:34,108 --> 00:09:34,774
signals.

239
00:09:34,954 --> 00:09:37,306
It is about a crew with hoses

240
00:09:37,306 --> 00:09:38,765
fighting the snow,

241
00:09:38,765 --> 00:09:40,387
one train at a time.

242
00:09:40,737 --> 00:09:43,455
Why does much of the Shinkansen track

243
00:09:43,455 --> 00:09:44,557
have no stones?

244
00:09:44,737 --> 00:09:47,317
Most railways lay their track on a

245
00:09:47,317 --> 00:09:49,973
bed of crushed rock called ballast.

246
00:09:50,153 --> 00:09:51,212
It works well,

247
00:09:51,212 --> 00:09:53,330
but trains shake the stones,

248
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:55,070
the track slowly moves,

249
00:09:55,070 --> 00:09:57,793
and crews have to fix it constantly.

250
00:09:57,973 --> 00:10:02,009
Many Shinkansen lines use slab track instead,

251
00:10:02,009 --> 00:10:04,700
rails fixed on concrete slabs,

252
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:08,468
with a layer of special mortar underneath.

253
00:10:08,648 --> 00:10:11,058
It stays in place much better,

254
00:10:11,058 --> 00:10:13,547
and needs far less maintenance.

255
00:10:13,897 --> 00:10:17,473
Slab track also has another advantage.

256
00:10:17,653 --> 00:10:18,765
At high speed,

257
00:10:18,765 --> 00:10:21,147
the air under a train can lift

258
00:10:21,147 --> 00:10:24,245
small stones of ballast and throw them,

259
00:10:24,245 --> 00:10:27,580
a problem sometimes called ballast flight.

260
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:30,384
Concrete slabs do not fly.

261
00:10:30,564 --> 00:10:32,723
The downside is cost.

262
00:10:32,903 --> 00:10:36,123
Slab track is more expensive to build,

263
00:10:36,123 --> 00:10:37,309
and once laid,

264
00:10:37,309 --> 00:10:39,766
it is harder to adjust if the

265
00:10:39,766 --> 00:10:40,868
ground moves.

266
00:10:41,217 --> 00:10:44,210
Japan began using slab track widely on

267
00:10:44,210 --> 00:10:47,911
the Sanyo Shinkansen in the nineteen seventies,

268
00:10:47,911 --> 00:10:50,273
and many later lines followed.

269
00:10:50,453 --> 00:10:52,844
For a railway that runs hundreds of

270
00:10:52,844 --> 00:10:54,756
trains a day with only a few

271
00:10:54,756 --> 00:10:56,873
hours at night for maintenance,

272
00:10:56,873 --> 00:10:59,264
a track that does not need constant

273
00:10:59,264 --> 00:11:01,518
fixing is worth its higher price.

274
00:11:01,867 --> 00:11:04,324
Why can a bullet train start braking

275
00:11:04,324 --> 00:11:06,848
before an earthquake shaking arrives?

276
00:11:07,028 --> 00:11:10,542
Japan has seismometers along the coast and

277
00:11:10,542 --> 00:11:11,881
along the lines.

278
00:11:12,061 --> 00:11:15,126
An earthquake sends two main waves.

279
00:11:15,306 --> 00:11:18,015
The fast P wave arrives first and

280
00:11:18,015 --> 00:11:18,836
is weaker.

281
00:11:19,016 --> 00:11:21,932
The slower S wave carries the strong

282
00:11:21,932 --> 00:11:22,580
shaking.

283
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:25,827
When the sensors detect the first wave,

284
00:11:25,827 --> 00:11:28,501
the system can cut power to trains

285
00:11:28,501 --> 00:11:32,904
automatically, and their emergency brakes start working,

286
00:11:32,904 --> 00:11:36,994
sometimes seconds before the strong shaking arrives.

287
00:11:37,174 --> 00:11:40,765
Japan even places seismometers like this one

288
00:11:40,765 --> 00:11:42,315
on the ocean floor,

289
00:11:42,315 --> 00:11:45,417
to catch offshore earthquakes earlier.

290
00:11:45,766 --> 00:11:47,452
In two thousand four,

291
00:11:47,452 --> 00:11:49,860
during the Chuetsu earthquake,

292
00:11:49,860 --> 00:11:53,152
a Shinkansen train traveling at about two

293
00:11:53,152 --> 00:11:56,041
hundred kilometers an hour derailed.

294
00:11:56,221 --> 00:11:58,654
It was the first derailment of a

295
00:11:58,654 --> 00:12:01,086
Shinkansen in passenger service.

296
00:12:01,266 --> 00:12:02,775
Nobody was killed.

297
00:12:02,955 --> 00:12:06,410
After that, railways added devices that keep

298
00:12:06,410 --> 00:12:09,237
a derailed train close to the rails,

299
00:12:09,237 --> 00:12:11,907
so it slides along them instead of

300
00:12:11,907 --> 00:12:12,928
tipping over.

301
00:12:13,279 --> 00:12:15,026
In March twenty eleven,

302
00:12:15,026 --> 00:12:18,825
the huge Tohoku earthquake struck while Shinkansen

303
00:12:18,825 --> 00:12:21,789
trains were running on the Tohoku line.

304
00:12:21,969 --> 00:12:24,204
The warning system cut power,

305
00:12:24,204 --> 00:12:27,365
and the trains in service stopped without

306
00:12:27,365 --> 00:12:30,525
any passenger being hurt in a derailment.

307
00:12:30,705 --> 00:12:32,557
In twenty twenty-two,

308
00:12:32,557 --> 00:12:37,320
another strong earthquake derailed a Tohoku Shinkansen

309
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:41,201
near Shiroishi, and again nobody was killed.

310
00:12:41,381 --> 00:12:43,373
A few seconds of warning,

311
00:12:43,373 --> 00:12:46,163
used by machines instead of humans,

312
00:12:46,163 --> 00:12:49,032
can be the difference between a stop

313
00:12:49,032 --> 00:12:50,227
and a disaster.

314
00:12:50,578 --> 00:12:53,261
Why is one Japanese bullet train painted

315
00:12:53,261 --> 00:12:54,200
bright yellow?

316
00:12:54,380 --> 00:12:56,322
It is called Doctor Yellow,

317
00:12:56,322 --> 00:12:58,408
and it is not for passengers.

318
00:12:58,588 --> 00:13:01,409
It is an inspection train that runs

319
00:13:01,409 --> 00:13:04,551
at full speed while measuring the track

320
00:13:04,551 --> 00:13:06,324
and the overhead wire,

321
00:13:06,324 --> 00:13:09,950
checking for tiny problems before they become

322
00:13:09,950 --> 00:13:10,756
dangerous.

323
00:13:10,936 --> 00:13:14,150
Its sensors can measure the track's alignment

324
00:13:14,150 --> 00:13:16,865
and the condition of the electric wire

325
00:13:16,865 --> 00:13:18,865
while running at high speed.

326
00:13:19,216 --> 00:13:22,456
Doctor Yellow did not follow a public

327
00:13:22,456 --> 00:13:25,872
timetable, so seeing one became a small

328
00:13:25,872 --> 00:13:27,273
legend in Japan.

329
00:13:27,453 --> 00:13:29,415
People said it brings good luck.

330
00:13:29,595 --> 00:13:32,777
JR Central retired its Doctor Yellow in

331
00:13:32,777 --> 00:13:34,980
January twenty twenty-five,

332
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:37,672
and JR West planned to retire its

333
00:13:37,672 --> 00:13:38,488
own later.

334
00:13:38,668 --> 00:13:41,377
Their job is moving to sensors fitted

335
00:13:41,377 --> 00:13:43,427
on regular passenger trains,

336
00:13:43,427 --> 00:13:45,843
which measure the line every day.

337
00:13:46,194 --> 00:13:48,811
That change shows the philosophy of the

338
00:13:48,811 --> 00:13:49,549
Shinkansen.

339
00:13:49,729 --> 00:13:53,097
Instead of waiting for something to break,

340
00:13:53,097 --> 00:13:55,743
measure everything, all the time.

341
00:13:55,923 --> 00:13:57,899
The goal is to find a problem

342
00:13:57,899 --> 00:13:59,534
when it is a millimeter,

343
00:13:59,534 --> 00:14:01,101
not when it is a meter.

344
00:14:01,281 --> 00:14:03,394
That is how you run a railway

345
00:14:03,394 --> 00:14:06,237
at three hundred kilometers an hour for

346
00:14:06,237 --> 00:14:08,642
decades with almost no accidents.

347
00:14:08,992 --> 00:14:11,842
Why couldn't the bullet train simply use

348
00:14:11,842 --> 00:14:14,123
Japan's existing railway tracks?

349
00:14:14,303 --> 00:14:17,837
Because most old Japanese lines use a

350
00:14:17,837 --> 00:14:21,275
narrow gauge, about one point oh six

351
00:14:21,275 --> 00:14:24,236
seven meters between the rails,

352
00:14:24,236 --> 00:14:27,388
which limits speed and stability.

353
00:14:27,568 --> 00:14:30,581
The Shinkansen was built from the start

354
00:14:30,581 --> 00:14:33,517
on separate lines with standard gauge,

355
00:14:33,517 --> 00:14:36,066
one point four three five meters,

356
00:14:36,066 --> 00:14:38,384
the same width used by most of

357
00:14:38,384 --> 00:14:40,315
Europe and North America.

358
00:14:40,495 --> 00:14:42,888
A wider track allows bigger,

359
00:14:42,888 --> 00:14:45,708
more stable trains at high speed.

360
00:14:46,059 --> 00:14:49,082
The new lines were also built without

361
00:14:49,082 --> 00:14:50,389
level crossings.

362
00:14:50,569 --> 00:14:52,167
No cars, no people,

363
00:14:52,167 --> 00:14:55,533
no animals crossing the tracks at street

364
00:14:55,533 --> 00:14:56,037
level.

365
00:14:56,217 --> 00:14:59,228
Bridges and tunnels carry the line over

366
00:14:59,228 --> 00:15:00,850
and under everything.

367
00:15:01,030 --> 00:15:03,381
That alone removes one of the most

368
00:15:03,381 --> 00:15:05,801
common causes of railway accidents.

369
00:15:05,981 --> 00:15:08,663
A bullet train never has to worry

370
00:15:08,663 --> 00:15:11,426
about a truck stuck on the tracks.

371
00:15:11,777 --> 00:15:14,628
Some lines, called mini-Shinkansen,

372
00:15:14,628 --> 00:15:16,420
use a different trick.

373
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:19,448
They widened old narrow gauge lines to

374
00:15:19,448 --> 00:15:22,971
standard gauge, so bullet trains could continue

375
00:15:22,971 --> 00:15:25,519
from the high speed line onto them

376
00:15:25,519 --> 00:15:26,643
at lower speed.

377
00:15:26,823 --> 00:15:29,232
But on its own dedicated lines,

378
00:15:29,232 --> 00:15:32,185
the Shinkansen lives in its own world,

379
00:15:32,185 --> 00:15:34,128
which is why it can be so

380
00:15:34,128 --> 00:15:35,449
fast and so safe.

381
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:37,297
So here are the ten.

382
00:15:37,477 --> 00:15:40,213
A long nose against the tunnel boom,

383
00:15:40,213 --> 00:15:42,493
and hoods at the tunnel doors.

384
00:15:42,673 --> 00:15:45,545
A pantograph copied from an owl,

385
00:15:45,545 --> 00:15:48,327
and cars sealed like airplanes.

386
00:15:48,507 --> 00:15:51,381
A body that leans for your coffee,

387
00:15:51,381 --> 00:15:53,749
sprinklers against the snow,

388
00:15:53,749 --> 00:15:55,524
track without stones,

389
00:15:55,524 --> 00:15:58,568
sensors that feel earthquakes first,

390
00:15:58,568 --> 00:16:01,780
a yellow train that checks everything,

391
00:16:01,780 --> 00:16:04,486
and a wider track built just for

392
00:16:04,486 --> 00:16:04,993
speed.

393
00:16:05,342 --> 00:16:07,390
The bullet train does not look like

394
00:16:07,390 --> 00:16:08,442
that to look fast.

395
00:16:08,622 --> 00:16:10,619
Every curve has a reason.

396
00:16:10,799 --> 00:16:13,865
If you want more machines explained part

397
00:16:13,865 --> 00:16:16,778
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

398
00:16:16,958 --> 00:16:18,917
And tell us in the comments which

399
00:16:18,917 --> 00:16:20,876
machine you want us to take apart

